article.scientific_purpose_and_applications:
The purpose of a radiation biosensor is to measure how living cells respond to the radiation they actually experience, not only how much energy a physical detector records. A dosimeter records deposited energy but does not show whether biology responds. Yeast is used because it shares DNA damage and repair mechanisms with human cells, so it can serve as a model for radiation risk to crew [1][3]. For LEIA, NASA states that the instrument will measure the radiation environment at the lunar South Pole and use yeast to study the combined effects of radiation and reduced gravity [1]. Planned strains include BioSentinel strains, strains with engineered DNA repair modifications, beta-carotene-producing strains intended to test antioxidant production, and strains with added radiation-resistance genes [1][4]. Applications described in the sources are:
- biological data on sublethal radiation effects that can inform human exploration risk assessment [1][3];
- testing of engineered radiation-resistant or antioxidant-producing organisms as possible crew countermeasures [1][4];
- relating cell responses to a measured radiation field at the same site and time [1][4].
